{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>Upon me be your curse, my son, etc.</b> The reason she said <b>my son</b> after she had been speaking with him is: that which you now let out of your mouth — and the construal of the word is: a curse that you curse, my son. This is along the lines of what they said of blessed memory regarding a blessing (Makkot 11a): the curse of a sage, even if conditional, etc. Therefore she was fearful, and it was she who caused the curse that issued from his mouth; and had she not said ״my son,״ it would have been heard as a curse that his father curses him." "<b>And it seemed to me</b> to give a reason for Rebecca's love for Jacob, because the eyes of all were turned toward her, anticipating what would issue from her — and from her emerged the wicked one, namely Esau. Now, by familiar nature, like parts love like parts, and this is apparent from examination. Therefore she revealed her mind that it is not so, and her deeds prove that her source is blessed, and the good portion is her portion, and her nature runs toward the love of good. And therefore you will find that the verse frequently says, in every action and outcome of hers, ״Jacob her son״ with no need (Genesis 27:6,8,11,13,15,17,42), and its intent is to declare that from her are the paths of the righteous, and his foundation too was built from her foundation, while the emergence of Esau was not from Rebecca's aspect, Heaven forbid." "There is also a sod in the matter, as is known to those who know grace (חן): for it is the impurity (זוהמת) of the holiness after its refinement in the fire of gevurah that is in the aspect of Isaac our father — and understand. And although we wrote above (Genesis 25:20) that its cause is the fact that she was Laban's sister, this matter would not be a cause for Rebecca's aspect, for from her it would be as her deeds, Heaven forbid. And this particular detail is among that which is capable of separating the impurity (זוהמא) on its own and making the line of the good stand on its own — that is, Jacob our father, whose form (שופריה) is from the form (שופריה) of the first man (Bava Metzia 84a). It is a sod — and understand."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:43:53.871867387Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}